Spot on, Kelp! That changelog memory from QS 6.52 to 6.53 is crucial.
We already tested scrollIntoView() in past iterations and explicitly removed it because force-scrolling disrupted Gemini's Single Page Application (SPA) state and messed up subsequent prompt submissions.
So when Captain Cozmo hit F5 + scroll, the scroll part was a technique we know carries real side effects—leaving F5 / Page Reload as the real untested variable.
🔍 Breakdown of What a Page Refresh Actually Demands:
DOM Token Destruction: Hitting F5 completely wipes the data-qs-gemini-response-bind attribute from the page's DOM.
Re-binding Requirement: After reloading, PPR would have to wait for Gemini's SPA to fully hydrate, locate the response text again using node._gemini_response_bind_text, and re-bind a fresh token before attempting to click the action row.
Transport Layer Changes: As Kelp noted, the Node bridge (PUPPETEER_NODE_SCRIPT) doesn't even have a page.reload() operation exposed right now! Adding one means touching transport code—which is strictly Luma's call.
The Null Hypothesis (The 30-Second Wait): Before we write complex reload/rebind routines, we haven't tested the simplest question of all: Does the button row simply mount on its own if left completely untouched for 15–30 seconds?
